#!/usr/bin/env python3
# burrow-cheatsheet: docs/cheatsheet.md, every function, function-like macro
# and variable in burrow's public headers on one line each.
#
#     tools/burrow-cheatsheet            write docs/cheatsheet.md
#     tools/burrow-cheatsheet --check    fail if docs/cheatsheet.md is stale
#
# A line is the declaration as the header has it, with the BURROW_ annotations
# taken out and the white space made single, and then a sentence. Where the
# function or variable has a Go original, the sentence is Go's synopsis of it:
# the first sentence of its doc comment, cut where go/doc's firstSentence cuts
# it. Where it has none, like arena_init or chan_make, it is the first sentence
# of the comment just above the declaration in the header, if there is one. The
# Go original is found by the naming rules, read from tools/burrow-coverage, so
# the two tools always agree on what maps to what.
#
# The page is grouped by header, in the order the headers sort, and within a
# header the declarations come in the order they are declared. A name declared
# twice, in the branches of an #if, is listed once, the first time. Names that
# begin with burrow__ or an underscore are burrow's own internals and are left
# out.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
#
# Go's Synopsis runs the sentence through its doc comment parser. This takes
# the brackets off a doc link like [Reader] or [io.EOF], which is what that
# parser prints for one, and otherwise leaves the text alone. A synopsis that
# isn't a paragraph, like a comment that starts with a code block, is left
# empty here, as Go leaves it.
#
# A macro is listed when it is spelled in lower case and takes arguments, the
# way http_handle_func is, since that is a function as far as a caller can
# tell. Types, constants and the other macros aren't listed.
#
# It needs Python 3 and GOROOT pointing at the Go tree burrow tracks, or a Go
# toolchain on PATH, the same as tools/burrow-coverage. The page names the Go
# version it was made from.
#
